ve; James has slightly less cash"], "rationale": "The fundamentals favor Republicans in a district with a four-point Republican lean, but the midterm environment, the open seat, and a Tossup consensus among ten raters offset that. Markets and the lone recent poll both point to a margin near even, so a slight Democratic edge is warranted without straying far from the fundamentals.", "watch": "Any fresh independent polling or a shift in outside spending against Mitchell could break the race out of tossup territory in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 73 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Open seat: Ashley Hinson (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Iowa's 2nd District is an open seat this cycle, with Republican incumbent Ashley Hinson leaving to run statewide. The district carries a four-point Republican lean, but the 2026 midterm environment appears favorable to Democrats, and both parties recruited credible nominees. Joe Mitchell has raised about $1.9 million, slightly outpacing Democrat Lindsay James, though he is drawing significant outside opposition spending and attacks over his lobbying work.\n\nExpert raters overwhelmingly call this a Tossup, with a couple leaning toward each side, and prediction markets give the Democrat a narrow edge. The only recent poll, from July, showed a one-point Democratic lead. With thin and dated polling, uncertainty is high, and the race sits essentially at even with a slight Democratic tilt.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5595, "margin": 0.94}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.5585, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6246, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.619, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5799, "margin": 1.8}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.557, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6133, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.6185, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem":